# Flow Choices

A `choice` routes one Flow component to one Action. It checks options
in source order, runs the first matching target, and uses a required
`otherwise` target when no option matches.

The fallback is routing logic. It is not error recovery.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-rc.1"}])
```

## Define An Echo Action

```elixir
defmodule FlowChoices.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_choices_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Define A Choice

Each `option` has a unique name, a native `condition`, an `action`, and
`params`. `otherwise` has an `action` and `params`.

```elixir
defmodule FlowChoices.Routing do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_choices_routing",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{tier: Zoi.atom()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "priority_data",
      action: FlowChoices.Echo,
      params: %{kind: :priority}

    step "bulk_data",
      action: FlowChoices.Echo,
      params: %{kind: :bulk}

    step "fallback_data",
      action: FlowChoices.Echo,
      params: %{kind: :standard}

    choice "route" do
      option "priority",
        condition:
          input(:tier) == :priority and
            select(result("priority_data"), :kind) == :priority,
        action: FlowChoices.Echo,
        params: %{route: :priority}

      option "bulk" do
        condition(
          input(:tier) == :bulk and
            select(result("bulk_data"), :kind) == :bulk
        )

        action(FlowChoices.Echo)
        params(%{route: :bulk})
      end

      otherwise action: FlowChoices.Echo,
                params: %{route: select(result("fallback_data"), :kind)}
    end

    output result("route")
  end
end
```

Result references in conditions and target parameters create static
dependencies. All three predecessor nodes run before `route` is ready.

This rule also applies to unselected options and to `otherwise`. An outer
predecessor failure prevents the Choice from becoming ready, even when the
failed result belongs to an unselected target. Jido cannot select a route and
then remove an edge from the already compiled graph.

```elixir
{:ok, dependencies} = Jido.Flow.dependencies(FlowChoices.Routing.flow())
%{
  references: ["bulk_data", "fallback_data", "priority_data"],
  effective: ["bulk_data", "fallback_data", "priority_data"]
} = dependencies["route"]
```

## Ordered First Match

Jido checks options in source order. The first true condition wins. Only the
selected target runs.

Keep branch-specific work inside the selected Action when that work must be
lazy. Choice targets cannot be Flow modules. Use a top-level `step` when a
separate Subflow boundary is required.

```elixir
{:ok, %{route: :priority}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :priority}, %{})

{:ok, %{route: :bulk}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :bulk}, %{})

{:ok, %{route: :standard}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :other}, %{})
```

## Use Native Conditions

The DSL captures these operators as Flow data:

- `==` and `!=` compare values.
- `<`, `<=`, `>`, and `>=` compare two numbers or two strings.
- `left in right` checks membership in a proper list.
- `left and right` requires both conditions.
- `left or right` requires one condition.
- `not condition` inverts one condition.

For example:

```text
flow do
  choice "route" do
    option "priority",
      condition: input(:tier) in [:gold, :platinum] and input(:blocked) != true,
      action: MyApp.Actions.Priority,
      params: %{account_id: input(:account_id)}

    otherwise action: MyApp.Actions.Standard,
              params: %{account_id: input(:account_id)}
  end
end
```

Conditions can read `input`, `context`, and prior `result` values. A missing
path resolves to `nil`. Arbitrary predicate functions are not accepted.

## `otherwise` Is Required

A Choice must contain at least one option and exactly one `otherwise`. Missing
or duplicate fallback declarations fail at compile time. Option names must be
unique.

## Fallback Is Not Error Recovery

`otherwise` runs only when no option condition is true. It does not run after a
selected target error.

```elixir
defmodule FlowChoices.Failing do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_choices_failing",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(_params, _context), do: {:error, :selected_target_failed}
end

defmodule FlowChoices.NoRecovery do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_choices_no_recovery",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    choice "route" do
      option "selected",
        condition: true == true,
        action: FlowChoices.Failing,
        params: %{}

      otherwise action: FlowChoices.Echo,
                params: %{route: :fallback}
    end

    output result("route")
  end
end

{:error, _error} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowChoices.NoRecovery, %{}, %{})
```

## Build A Choice At Runtime

`Jido.Flow.Builder` uses normal functions to build the same canonical Choice
data:

```elixir
alias Jido.Flow.Builder

builder =
  Builder.new(name: "builder_choice")
  |> Builder.choice(
    :route,
    [
      Builder.option(
        :yes,
        Builder.eq(Builder.input(:kind), Builder.value(:yes)),
        FlowChoices.Echo,
        %{route: Builder.value(:yes)}
      )
    ],
    Builder.fallback(FlowChoices.Echo, %{route: Builder.value(:no)})
  )
  |> Builder.output(Builder.result(:route))

{:ok, built_flow} = Builder.build(builder)
%Jido.Flow{name: "builder_choice"} = built_flow
```

## Step Through A Choice

A Choice compiles to one native Step. Its option targets are not separate
runnables.

```elixir
{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.start(FlowChoices.Routing, %{tier: :priority}, %{})

{:ok, _predecessors, execution} = Jido.Exec.wave(execution)

[runnable] = Jido.Exec.ready(execution)
{:ok, %Runic.Workflow.Runnable{status: :completed}, execution} =
  Jido.Exec.step(execution, runnable)

{:ok, execution} = Jido.Exec.continue(execution)
{:ok, %{route: :priority}} = Jido.Exec.result(execution)
```

